is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in the control-plane, not a physical core link. Host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2) can reach its default gateway (switch_dist_1_1, 10.1.1.1) and that switch can reach router_core_1 over the uplink (172.16.0.7 -> 172.16.0.6), proving local cabling is fine. Host_2_1_1_1 can reach the server farm (10.200.0.0/24), so the server side and the rest of the core are healthy.

On router_core_1, FRR is down: systemctl shows frr.service Active: failed, and its routing table has only directly connected /31 links (no OSPF-learned routes). “show ip ospf neighbor” on router_core_1 reports ospfd is not running. In contrast, router_core_2 and router_core_3 have healthy OSPF adjacencies and routes to 10.200.0.0/24. Therefore, traffic from 10.1.1.0/24 sent via switch_dist_1_1 toward router_core_1 hits a router that has no route to 10.200.0.0/24 and is dropped, creating the observed outage from host_1_1_1_1 only.

Localization: router_core_1, FRR/ospfd daemon (control-plane). All core interfaces on router_core_1 are up (connected routes present), so there is no physical link failure.

Root cause: FRR (ospfd) is not running on router_core_1, leaving it without OSPF routes to the server farm.

Corrective fix: Restore FRR on router_core_1 (start/restart frr service, fix any config/startup errors), verify OSPF neighbors with router_core_2 and router_core_3 form, and confirm 10.200.0.0/24 appears in router_core_1’s RIB/FIB. No cable work is required. === END ===